<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hagar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Agar, Hajar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hagar is an Egyptian woman in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, the concubine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> and the mother of his firstborn son Ishmael. Genesis says that Sarah, believing herself barren, gave her to Abraham, that tension followed, and that Hagar fled into the desert, where an angel told her to return and promised her many descendants. After Isaac's birth she and Ishmael were sent away and, according to Genesis, God showed her a well. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> does not name her, but Islamic tradition describes Hajar as a maid who ran seven times between Safa and Marwah and found the Zamzam well, an act recalled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a> rites. Ishmael is generally taken as the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hagar.jpg" alt="Hagar" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: François-Joseph Navez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:François-Joseph_Navez_-_Agar_et_Ismaël_dans_le_désert_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
